---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 1762-1802";
const lessonScripture = "Ps 119:105; Mt 5:8; Rom 8:5-8; 1 Tim 1:5-7";
---
<LessonLayout title="The Passions and Moral Conscience" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">If you feel that something is wrong but do it anyway &mdash; what failed? Your feeling or your decision?</p>

<p>
A young man named Carlos had a heated argument with his brother. The argument escalated. Carlos shouted hurtful words he didn&rsquo;t really mean, then stormed out slamming the door. Hours later, calm again, he realized they weren&rsquo;t even disagreeing about anything important. His anger had spoken for him. Anger is a passion &mdash; not bad in itself. Jesus himself felt anger in the Temple (Jn 2:13-17). But when anger rules instead of reason, it can destroy. Passions are like fire: they can warm your home or burn it down. Conscience is what decides when to stoke the fire and when to put it out.
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>The Passions: Enemies or Allies?</h2>

<p>
The passions (also called emotions or feelings) are the natural movements of the soul toward good or evil. We feel joy when we attain something good; sadness when we lose something; anger when we are attacked; fear before danger. All of this is human and was created by God (CCC 1762-1764).
</p>

<p>
The Catechism teaches that &laquo;passions are evil only when the acts they cause are evil. They are good when they contribute to a good action&raquo; (CCC 1768). In other words: there are no &laquo;bad&raquo; emotions by themselves. What matters is what we do with them. Feeling anger is not a sin; insulting or hitting someone is. Feeling desire is not a sin; coveting what belongs to others is.
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity</h4>
<p>Think of the last time you felt a strong emotion. What did you do with it? Did it help you or harm you? Now imagine someone says something hurtful to you. Your anger pushes you to respond with more anger. What other choice do you have?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Conscience: The Inner Voice</h2>

<p>
Conscience is &laquo;man&rsquo;s most secret core, and his sanctuary. There he is alone with God whose voice echoes in his depths&raquo; (CCC 1776). It is the capacity God has given us to distinguish good from evil. It is not a vague feeling or an emotional intuition: it is a judgment of reason that applies moral truth to a concrete situation (CCC 1778).
</p>

<p>
Conscience tells us: &laquo;what you are about to do is right&raquo; or &laquo;this is wrong.&raquo; It is not always easy to hear, especially when our emotions are stirred up. That is why the formation of conscience is essential. Just as a musician trains their ear to distinguish notes, the Christian trains their conscience to distinguish good from evil (CCC 1783-1785).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection</h4>
<p>St. Paul speaks of those whose conscience is &laquo;seared&raquo; (1 Tim 4:2) &mdash; hardened by repeated sin until they no longer feel remorse. Do you know any practice that helps keep your conscience sensitive to good and evil? Silence? Prayer? Frequent confession?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Certain, Doubtful, and Erroneous Conscience</h2>

<p>
The Catechism distinguishes three states of conscience (CCC 1787-1792):
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Certain conscience</strong>: I am sure this is good or evil. I must act according to this judgment.</li>
  <li><strong>Doubtful conscience</strong>: I don&rsquo;t know if this is good or evil. Before acting, I must seek the truth.</li>
  <li><strong>Erroneous conscience</strong>: I believe something is good when it is actually evil. If the error is invincible (I had no way to know), I am not guilty. But if it is due to negligence or willful ignorance, I am responsible.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Here we see the link with passions. A person dominated by anger can convince themselves they were &laquo;in the right&raquo; to shout. Their conscience is clouded. Passion has distorted their judgment. That is why we cannot make important decisions when we are emotionally agitated (CCC 1792-1794).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Deepening</h4>
<p>St. Thomas teaches that an erroneous conscience obliges, even if it is wrong. That is, if you sincerely believe something is required (even if you are mistaken), you must follow it. Conscience is the ultimate subjective norm. But you have an obligation to form it well. You cannot excuse yourself with &laquo;I didn&rsquo;t know&raquo; if you had the means to know (cf. CCC 1790-1791).</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>How Passions Affect Conscience</h2>

<p>
Passions are not evil, but they can cloud the judgment of conscience in several ways (CCC 1767-1770, 1792-1793):
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Anger</strong> makes us see the other as an enemy, distorting reality.</li>
  <li><strong>Fear</strong> prevents us from doing what we know we should.</li>
  <li><strong>Lust</strong> reduces another person to an object of pleasure.</li>
  <li><strong>Greed</strong> makes us justify the unjustifiable for money.</li>
  <li><strong>Pride</strong> prevents us from recognizing our mistakes.</li>
</ul>

<p>
But when conscience is well-formed, the opposite happens. The virtuous person feels the same passions, but these are &laquo;ordered&raquo; by reason enlightened by faith. The brave person feels fear, but their conscience tells them they must act, and they do. The merciful person feels indignation at injustice, but responds with constructive action, not vengeance (CCC 1796-1802).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Deepening</h4>
<p>The Desert Fathers spoke of the &laquo;guardianship of the heart&raquo;: being attentive to inner movements. St. Ignatius of Loyola developed this in the Rules for the Discernment of Spirits: learning to distinguish when an interior movement comes from God (consolation) and when from the enemy (desolation). This discernment is a skill that is trained and helps our passions not to govern our conscience.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Biblical Connection: The Pure Heart That Sees God</h2>

<blockquote>
<p>&laquo;Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God&raquo; (Mt 5:8).</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Purity of heart is not about having no emotions; it is about having an ordered heart where God is at the center. St. Paul describes the inner conflict: &laquo;I do not do the good I want, but the evil I do not want is what I keep on doing&raquo; (Rom 7:19). Everyone experiences this struggle between what we know is right and what our passions push us to do. The good news is that Christ has set us free. &laquo;If the Son sets you free, you will be free indeed&raquo; (Jn 8:36). In Him, grace heals our passions and orders our conscience.
</p>

<p>
The psalmist knew this well: &laquo;Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path&raquo; (Ps 119:105). God&rsquo;s Word illuminates conscience and orders the passions.
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Which emotion do you most often lose control of? Anger, fear, sadness, desire?</li>
  <li>Have you ever made an important decision while emotionally agitated? How did it turn out?</li>
  <li>How do you currently form your conscience? Do you read Scripture, consult the Catechism, speak with a spiritual director?</li>
  <li>What role does prayer play in forming your conscience?</li>
  <li>Is there an area of your life where you know you should act differently but your emotions prevent you?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Key Idea</h2>

<div class="callout">
<p><strong>Key Idea:</strong> Passions are not enemies but allies when conscience orders them. Forming conscience and ordering the heart is the work of a lifetime.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Cultural Treasure</h2>

<p>
<strong>Kierkegaard&rsquo;s &laquo;Knight of Faith&raquo;</strong> &mdash; Kierkegaard speaks of the &laquo;knight of faith&raquo;: one who, like Abraham, is willing to obey God even when reason and emotions scream against it. Abraham felt terror at taking Isaac to Mount Moriah, but his conscience, formed by faith, told him to trust. It was not blind emotion: it was a conscience illuminated by faith that ordered his passions.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Michelangelo&rsquo;s &laquo;The Last Judgment&raquo; in the Sistine Chapel</strong> &mdash; In this masterpiece, human emotions are represented with overwhelming intensity: desire, fear, hope, despair. But everything is ordered around Christ, who is the center of all. That is the vocation of our passions: to be ordered around Christ.
</p>

</section>

</LessonLayout>
